 ## Overview
-TSDF Block Transform Coding is a lossy compression method for Truncated Signed Distance Function (TSDF) volumes, designed to enable real-time streaming of dynamic implicit surfaces. It solves the problem of efficiently encoding sparse, locally smooth TSDF data by decomposing the volume into fixed-size voxel blocks, applying a 3D Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT) to concentrate energy in low frequencies, and entropy coding the retained coefficients. It is distinguished from its siblings by its use of a fixed, data-independent 3D DCT (avoiding the overhead of learned transforms like KLT) and its specific strategy of grouping coefficients by DCT index across all active blocks to optimize probability models for range coding [S1].
